      "rank": 1,
      "ballot_id": "B024",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexandre Blais", "Robert J. Schoelkopf", "Steven M. Girvin"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit QED established superconducting artificial atoms as a controllable platform for quantum optics and quantum information, opening a new experimental regime with broad influence.",
        "attribution": "Blais developed the theoretical framework; Schoelkopf led key experimental realizations; Girvin helped shape the theory of the field. The proposed trio captures complementary foundational contributions.",
        "maturity": "The platform has been reproduced across laboratories and supported decades of increasingly precise experiments. Its scientific importance is established independently of fault-tolerant computing.",
        "uncertainties": ["The boundary between foundational circuit-QED work and the broader development of superconducting qubits is difficult to draw."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexander A. Belavin", "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov", "Alexander M. Polyakov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The exact solution of two-dimensional critical phenomena through conformal field theory transformed statistical physics and influenced quantum field theory well beyond its original setting.",
        "attribution": "Belavin, Polyakov, and Zamolodchikov jointly introduced the decisive framework and its use in classifying critical behavior; credit among them is unusually well aligned with the stated discovery.",
        "maturity": "The theory has had exceptional longevity and extensive application. Its standing does not depend on a future experimental test.",
        "uncertainties": ["The nominated scope joins a mathematical framework with applications across many systems, so the prize citation would need to define the contribution carefully."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B012",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michael Berry", "Yakir Aharonov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm and Berry phases revealed that quantum phase and gauge structure have observable consequences, with enduring impact across quantum mechanics and condensed matter.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's work established the physical significance of the vector potential; Berry identified the geometric phase in adiabatic evolution. The two contributions are distinct and complementary.",
        "maturity": "The effects are experimentally established and have become standard tools in modern physics.",
        "uncertainties": ["The broad reach of geometric and topological phases makes a sharply bounded prize citation challenging."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B029",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks brought atomic timekeeping to a regime of extraordinary precision and opened new tests of fundamental physics, including searches for variation in constants and relativistic effects.",
        "attribution": "Katori's lattice approach and Ye's major experimental development are central, complementary contributions to the realization of these clocks.",
        "maturity": "Independent implementations and sustained gains in precision establish the result firmly; the technology continues to improve.",
        "uncertainties": ["The distinction between the clock invention and the broader advances in optical frequency measurement could affect the scope of recognition."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B022",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Carlton M. Caves", "H. Jeff Kimble", "Roman Schnabel"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light made quantum noise a controllable resource for precision measurement and has enabled measurements beyond the standard quantum limit.",
        "attribution": "Caves provided foundational analysis of quantum limits; Kimble advanced experimental quantum optics and squeezed-state methods; Schnabel helped establish high-performance squeezed-light use in interferometry.",
        "maturity": "The effect is well confirmed, and squeezed light is now used in gravitational-wave observatories.",
        "uncertainties": ["The development spans theory, early demonstrations, and later instrument integration, so the most appropriate division of credit is not uniquely determined."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Albrecht Karle", "Francis Halzen", "Christian Spiering"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "IceCube's detection of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos opened a new observational channel on the distant, energetic universe and established neutrino astronomy as an empirical field.",
        "attribution": "Halzen was central to the scientific conception and leadership; Karle and Spiering made major contributions to detector development and the collaboration's formation. Their roles represent different parts of a large effort.",
        "maturity": "The discovery has been followed by a growing body of events and source studies, supporting the reality of the astrophysical flux.",
        "uncertainties": ["The observatory was built by a large international collaboration, and the division of credit among individual contributors is inherently difficult."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B018",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Immanuel Bloch", "Markus Greiner", "Peter Zoller"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical-lattice experiments made controlled quantum simulation of many-body systems possible and produced landmark demonstrations of quantum phase transitions and correlated behavior.",
        "attribution": "Zoller's theoretical proposals and Bloch's and Greiner's experimental contributions form a strong core of the achievement, though the field includes many essential groups.",
        "maturity": "The platform has been independently developed and has generated a substantial body of reproducible physics.",
        "uncertainties": ["The discovery and maturation unfolded across multiple laboratories, and it is difficult to identify a small set of contributors that represents the full field."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexei Kitaev", "John Preskill", "Sergey Bravyi"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Topological quantum error correction gave a concrete route to protecting quantum information and shaped the theory and design of scalable quantum processors.",
        "attribution": "Kitaev's surface-code work, Preskill's influential development of quantum error correction, and Bravyi's contributions to code theory and implementation make a plausible theoretical grouping.",
        "maturity": "The concepts are firmly established, and small-scale error correction is experimentally demonstrated, but large-scale fault-tolerant operation remains unachieved.",
        "uncertainties": ["It remains unclear how much of the ultimate significance will attach specifically to surface codes versus the broader field of quantum error correction."]
      }
    }
  ]
